# The Substrate — reviewer 2 (obligation reachability)
VERDICT: FINDINGS
COUNT: 10
## Findings
1. Documentation IS Being — The only normative clause, “a design must never reward it,” names no actor responsible for producing the durable artifact or deciding what activity requires one — “a diagnostic session confirms there is no defect and changes nothing,” leaving the session agent, framework designer, and operator each able to disclaim the record.
2. Deferral = Deletion — “Anything worth keeping” must be captured now, but whether evidence is worth keeping can become knowable only afterward — “an anomalous log line looks harmless during a healthy run and becomes the sole clue after tomorrow’s outage.”
3. Observation Needs Structure — The article requires an unskippable artifact whenever “a system is supposed to observe X,” but assigns creation of that artifact to no role — “a specification adds a duty to watch delivery latency, while neither the feature author, observer, nor platform owner is named as owing the gate.”
4. The Agent Is Always Reachable — “Actually RESPONDS” has no response deadline or decidable liveness test, so compliance cannot be determined when the floor is evaluated — “a healthy session is silent during a ten-minute tool operation and is respawned as wedged, duplicating work and increasing the resource pressure.”
5. The Agent Is Always Reachable — Its command that “any session denied / held / killed for a RESOURCE reason emits ONE… user notice” conflicts with Interaction’s Near-Silent Notifications and Self-Heal Before Notify — “a background session is briefly pressure-held and automatically recovers,” for which Substrate requires a notice while Interaction forbids one.
6. Name the Gravity Wells — “Enumerate the predictable self-deceptions” names no responsible enumerator and supplies no point at which a trap becomes predictably enumerable — “a routine model upgrade introduces a new helpfulness bias that cannot be named until instances have already exhibited it.”
7. Architectural Agency in the Gap — The article describes what the agent is but assigns no actor any action, trigger, or acceptance condition, so it cannot be complied with — “training pulls toward agreeing with a reviewer while the evidence supports dissent,” and the article does not say who must select, build, or invoke which aligning structure.
8. Sovereignty — “If yes, act” does not identify the ownership level and conflicts with Building’s Ownership-Gated Side Effects, under which a non-conversation-owner “never acts locally” — “a standby machine has the agent’s own cloud token but receives work for a topic currently owned by the laptop”; Sovereignty orders action while Building orders forwarding or queuing.
9. The Operator Channel Is Sacred — The claim that a missed inbound “pause” is benign makes its delivery-first rule unsafe when applied literally — “pause before sending the remaining 500 emails” is phrased outside the exact-match set, reaches a busy agent instead of stopping the sender, and more irreversible messages leave before the agent handles it.
10. Session Input Is a Principal — At the moment untagged input arrives, the session lacks the very provenance required to decide whether it is a genuine driver instruction or impersonation — “the real driver sends an urgent untagged correction”; accepting it violates principal verification, while refusing it discards legitimate operator control.
